Spec sketch below. No code yet; I'd like your call on the open points
at the end before writing the skill.

* Input

Session transcripts in ~/.claude/projects/-home-claude-lab-default-jj/,
one <uuid>.jsonl per session, plus <uuid>/subagents/agent-*.jsonl.
Records are user/assistant/system/attachment; assistant content holds
text, thinking and tool_use blocks, and tool_result comes back on the
user side. Six sessions exist today.

These only outlive a jail rebuild once "[PATCH 0/2] jails: Persist
lab-claude's Claude state" [1] lands, so that series is a prerequisite
in practice.

jq, python3 and claude-code-log are not installed in the jail. Either
we add jq, or subagents read the JSONL directly. I lean toward jq: a
pre-pass that drops thinking and long tool output keeps each subagent
small.

* What it looks for

Friction signals, cheapest first:

- tool calls that failed and were retried in a different form (e.g.
  GNU flags on FreeBSD, then the BSD form);
- your corrections ("no", "don't", "instead") and undo operations
  (jj op restore, jj undo, jj abandon of fresh work);
- steps the model did by hand that a skill or script nearly covers,
  i.e. the same command sequence across several sessions;
- instructions in CLAUDE.md or a skill that the transcripts show being
  ignored, contradicted, or never needed (candidates for delete).

A concrete one from this pass: process-email's trunk rebase swept up my
mail outbox commits because mail@upstream had moved since the last
pass, so "~ mail@upstream::" no longer excluded them. I undid it with
jj op restore and rebased the outbox first. That is exactly the kind
of thing /self-improve should surface as a patch to the skill.

* How it works

1. Pick sessions: those since the last run. The last run is recorded
   as a file (e.g. .self-improve/last-run with the newest session id
   reviewed), not inferred from model output, per "Own the loop".
2. Fan out one read-only subagent per session. Each writes findings to
   a named file of known shape: signal, evidence (session id + record
   uuid), affected component, proposed action
   (add/update/refactor/delete).
3. The parent validates those files, merges duplicates across
   sessions, and ranks by recurrence. One-off friction is reported but
   not acted on.
4. For the top findings, the parent writes changes as jj commits on
   trunk@upstream, one logical change each, and posts them to the list
   as a series. Nothing is edited in place; you review everything.
   Findings not acted on go in the cover letter.

* Shape

.claude/skills/self-improve/SKILL.md with
"disable-model-invocation: true", matching process-email. Per the
docs that keeps it out of the model's context and user-only.
(Note that "user-invocable: false" is the opposite: hidden from /,
still model-callable.)

