Built into Claude Code:

- /insights reports friction across recent sessions and suggests
  CLAUDE.md additions. Reports only. Cheap first pass worth running
  by hand to see whether it finds what we care about.
- /fewer-permission-prompts mines transcripts and writes a settings
  allowlist. Same pattern as ours, but it writes in place.
- Hooks: SessionEnd gets transcript_path, so a hook could append each
  finished session to a queue file. That is a cleaner step 1 than
  scanning by mtime.
- Auto memory writes notes under ~/.claude/projects/*/memory/ without
  review. I'd turn it off (autoMemoryEnabled: false) so learning goes
  through patches only.
- Anthropic's skill-creator (github.com/anthropics/skills) drafts,
  evals and revises skills. Usable off-the-shelf when a finding calls
  for a new skill.

Community:

- claude-reflect (github.com/BayramAnnakov/claude-reflect): hooks
  catch correction phrases into a queue; /reflect walks them with
  approve/edit/skip; --scan-history mines old transcripts;
  /reflect-skills drafts skills from repeated requests. Closest to
  what you describe. Borrow the queue, history scan and dedupe; skip
  its in-place CLAUDE.md writes.
- reflect-skill-claude (github.com/hansvangent/reflect-skill-claude):
  extract, then audit config against actual behavior, then compress
  CLAUDE.md. The audit and compress phases map to our "delete"
  action.
- claude-code-log (github.com/daaain/claude-code-log): JSONL to
  Markdown/HTML. Needs Python; jq covers what we need.

Research:

- ExpeL (arXiv 2308.10144) keeps a lesson list edited with
  add/upvote/downvote/edit rather than append-only. That is the right
  model for CLAUDE.md: revise rules, don't just pile them up.
- Voyager (arXiv 2305.16291) only admits skills that pass
  verification. For us: a proposed script or skill change ships with
  the test that shows it works.
- Agent Workflow Memory (arXiv 2409.07429) turns repeated action
  sequences into reusable workflows. That is the "same commands across
  sessions" signal above.

